# Async and Event Hygiene

## Coroutine or `async`? Pick by what the work is

| Work | Use |
|------|-----|
| Frame-timed visuals, sequencing tied to a MonoBehaviour's lifetime (tweens, reveal sequences, timed VFX) | **Coroutine** — dies with the object, no lifetime guard needed, cached yields allocate nothing |
| I/O and anything that returns a value (network, asset load, server round-trip) | **`async` / `Awaitable`** with `destroyCancellationToken` |
| Waiting a fixed time inside either | Coroutine: cached `WaitForSeconds`; async: `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync` / `MainThreadDelay` |

Do not mix the two inside one flow (a coroutine that starts a task and polls `IsCompleted`, or a task that starts a coroutine and awaits a flag). If a sequence needs a server result in the middle, the whole sequence is `async`.

## No `Task.Run` — ever

WebGL has no threads (see the webgl no-threads rule). `Task.Run`, `ConfigureAwait(false)`, and thread-pool continuations either fail or silently run on the main thread. Use coroutines or `Awaitable`/main-thread `async` for time-sliced work.

## No `Task.Delay` — ever

`Task.Delay` uses a thread-pool timer that never fires on WebGL — awaited delays hang forever. Use `MainThreadDelay.WaitAsync` / `WaitMillisecondsAsync` or `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync` with a `CancellationToken` (e.g. `destroyCancellationToken`).

## `async void` is banned (one exception)

`async void` swallows exceptions and can't be awaited or cancelled. The only allowed use is a thin adapter for a Unity message/UI callback that immediately awaits a `Task`-returning method and catches at the top.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — fire-and-forget, exceptions vanish
private async void LoadProfile() { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — Task-returning core, adapter only at the event boundary
private async void OnLoadClicked()  // UI callback boundary
{
    try
    {
        await LoadProfileAsync();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        // destroyed or cancelled mid-load — expected, not an error
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        Debug.LogException(e);
    }
}

private async Task LoadProfileAsync() { ... }
```

## Async work must respect object lifetime

Play mode can exit, and objects can be destroyed, while an `await` is pending. Handle that with cancellation, not with post-await null checks.

Thread the MonoBehaviour's `destroyCancellationToken` through every awaited call. On destruction the pending await throws `OperationCanceledException`, the method unwinds, and no continuation reaches a dead object:

```csharp
private async Task RefreshAsync()
{
    var profile = await FetchAsync(destroyCancellationToken);
    _nameLabel.text = profile.Name;   // unreachable if destroyed mid-fetch
}
```

When the awaited API takes no token (a `UnityWebRequest` poll loop, a third-party task), check the token at the yield point instead:

```csharp
while (!request.isDone)
{
    destroyCancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    await Task.Yield();
}
```

Do **not** write `if (this == null) return` after an await. It is a silent bail on a path that should either cancel or fail loudly: without it, a continuation touching a destroyed object throws `MissingReferenceException` with a stack trace — the fail-fast behaviour every other rule asks for.

## Events: subscribe and unsubscribe symmetrically

Every `+=` / `AddListener` has a matching `-=` / `RemoveListener` in the mirrored lifecycle method: `Awake`↔`OnDestroy`, `OnEnable`↔`OnDisable`.

```csharp
private void OnEnable()
{
    GameEvents.RoundEnded += OnRoundEnded;
}

private void OnDisable()
{
    GameEvents.RoundEnded -= OnRoundEnded;
}
```

- **Static events** must also be cleared in the project's static-reset hub (see the unity domain-reload rule) — domain reload is disabled, so stale handlers survive play sessions.
- Subscribe with method groups, not lambdas — a lambda can't be unsubscribed and leaks the subscriber.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Coroutine for frame-timed visuals, `async` for I/O and value-returning work; not mixed in one flow
- [ ] No `Task.Run`, `Task.Delay`, or thread-pool APIs
- [ ] No `async void` except top-level Unity/UI callback adapters with try/catch
- [ ] `destroyCancellationToken` threaded through awaits in MonoBehaviours; no `this == null` guards after await
- [ ] Every subscribe has a symmetric unsubscribe; no lambda subscriptions
- [ ] New static events registered in the static-reset hub
